====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To behave [[violently]]; to [[rage]].
# {{lb|en|slang|transitive}} To [[swindle]] or [[rob]] [[violently]].
# {{lb|en|AU|slang|transitive}} To [[search]] a [[prisoner]] or a prisoner's [[cell]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To spring; to leap; to bound, rear, or prance; to move swiftly or violently.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=V|stanza=28|passage=Their bridles they would champe, / And trampling the fine element would fiercely '''rampe'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Chesterton Innocence of Father Brown|chapter=The Sign of the Broken Sword|passage=I've seen a '''ramping''' equestrian statue of General St. Clare on the Embankment.}}
#* {{RQ:C. S. Lewis Lion|passage=The shield was the colour of silver and across it there '''ramped''' a red lion, as bright as a ripe strawberry at the moment when you pick it.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To climb, like a plant; to creep up.
#* {{RQ:Parkinson Theatrum Botanicum|chapter=''Lathyrus.'' The Great Wilde Cicheling or Pease Everlasting.|page=1062|passage=''Lathyrus ſylveſtris flore luteo.'' Tare everlaſting. This '''ramping''' vvilde Vetch or Tare as the country people call it, becauſe it is the moſt pernicious herbe that can grovv on the earth, for corne or any other good herbe that it ſhall grovv by, killing and ſtrangling them: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Ray Wisdom|passage=With claspers and tendrils, they [plants] catch hold, {{...}} and so '''ramping''' upon trees, {{...}}they mount up to a great height.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To stand in a [[rampant]] position.
#* {{RQ:Malory Le Morte Darthur|book=IX|chapter=i|passage=And that lyon gaped wyde and came vpon hym '''raumppynge''' to haue slayne hym.|translation=And that lion gaped wide and came upon him '''ramping''' to have slain him.|brackets=on|termlang=en}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To (cause to) [[change]] value, often at a steady rate.
# To adapt a piece of iron to the woodwork of a gate.

